# Single environment source for the whole system — copy to .env (gitignored) and
# fill in. Python loads it (pipeline/settings.py), and setup.sh + Vite
# read it too. Real shell environment variables override these.
# Values are read literally (no shell or ${VAR} expansion), so passwords with
# special characters need no quoting.

# Backing store. UNSET → local SQLite file under each store's dir (dev/CI/solo).
# Set to a shared PostgreSQL database to point the whole team at one store.
# SQLite and PostgreSQL are the supported store dialects.
#   Supabase: use the TRANSACTION pooler (port 6543), not the session pooler
#   (5432). Session mode caps a tenant at ~15 client connections, which a few
#   app instances + reload workers exhaust; transaction mode multiplexes far more.
#   The engine is configured for it (NullPool + prepared statements disabled).
# TRIAGE_STORE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Emergency override: let a checkout behind the store's schema version write
# anyway (downgrades the refusal to a warning). Leave unset normally.
# TRIAGE_STORE_ALLOW_STALE=1

# Emergency override: let a process whose TRIAGE_REPO differs from the store's
# stamped repo write to the activity log anyway. Leave unset normally.
# TRIAGE_STORE_ALLOW_FOREIGN_REPO=1

# Upstream repo + bot identity. REQUIRED — pipeline/settings.py has no defaults,
# so the system never silently acts against the wrong repo/identity.
TRIAGE_REPO=owner/name
TRIAGE_BOT_LOGIN=your-github-app-login

# Repo presentation. Both OPTIONAL. The default branch is discovered from GitHub
# and cached per process; set it to skip discovery. Display name is the project's
# human name, shown in the app tab and headings (default: the repo short name).
# TRIAGE_DEFAULT_BRANCH=main
# TRIAGE_DISPLAY_NAME=

# External code-review provider whose verdict gates a clean merge. OPTIONAL —
# defaults to "none" (no external-review requirement; a PR clears the review bar
# on CI + mergeable alone). Set to "greptile" to require the confidence-score bar;
# TRIAGE_REVIEW_THRESHOLD overrides the pass score (greptile default 5).
# TRIAGE_REVIEW_PROVIDER=greptile
# TRIAGE_REVIEW_THRESHOLD=5

# Repository policy profile (policy data). OPTIONAL — path to a JSON file
# holding the triaged repository's policy vocabulary (subsystem taxonomy,
# risk tiers, CODEOWNERS gating); see profile.example.json for the shape.
# Unset → a generic default: every subsystem classifies as "other", risk
# ranking knows only the shared supply-chain surface, and nothing is
# CODEOWNERS-gated. Relative paths resolve against the repo root; the real
# profile lives beside this file as the gitignored profile.json.
# Match terms are lowercase regexes (the matched text is lowercased).
# The harness PR-template sections and VERIFY full-suite adapter come from the
# profile's harness and verify_suite sections.
# dependency_manifests also gates the VERIFY sandbox refusal — narrowing it weakens a security check.
# test_paths patterns are also compiled as JS RegExp — keep to the shared regex subset.
# TRIAGE_PROFILE=profile.json

# The bot's GitHub App: numeric app id + private key, by PATH (the PEM stays a
# file, not inline). Both REQUIRED for live writes — pipeline/get-bot-token.sh
# mints installation tokens from them, targeting the app's installation on the
# TRIAGE_REPO owner's account. Without them every write runs dry.
# TRIAGE_BOT_APP_ID=12345
# TRIAGE_BOT_KEY_FILE=~/.config/your-app/private-key.pem
#
# The 🛡️ Alerts tab (code scanning / Dependabot / secret scanning) needs no
# extra variables — alert reads and dismissals use this same App identity.
# Grant the App the three alert read/write repository permissions upstream;
# a missing permission just marks that source unavailable in the tab.

# Verify worker (a sandbox machine). Set TRIAGE_VERIFY_WORKER=1 on each machine
# that has the Docker sandbox and has prepared its own base image
# (sandbox/README.md has the machine checklist). Any number may run one: the
# queue claim is a compare-and-swap, so two workers never pick up the same PR,
# and each machine holds its own pin. Every other app serves the queue API only.
# TRIAGE_VERIFY_AUTOHUNT=1 additionally turns that worker's idle time into the
# security/verify hunter. The scratch root holds
# base clones, patches, and exclusion sets; it must live under $HOME on
# macOS+Colima (virtiofs shares only $HOME) and defaults to a per-repo
# directory. The probe deny-list names THIS machine's sensitive host services
# (host:port, comma-separated) the sandbox boot probe must fail to reach;
# unset keeps sandbox/boot-probe.sh's built-in default.
# TRIAGE_VERIFY_WORKER=1
# TRIAGE_VERIFY_AUTOHUNT=1
# TRIAGE_VERIFY_SCRATCH=~/.pr-triage-verify/owner-name
# TRIAGE_VERIFY_PROBE_DENY=192.168.5.2:3100,host.docker.internal:3100

# Contributor-push identity: the machine user that pushes autofix commits to PR
# head branches. A GitHub USER account (not the App in TRIAGE_BOT_LOGIN) holding
# push access on TRIAGE_REPO — pushing to a fork head branch rides the PR's
# "Allow edits from maintainers", which grants push to maintainer users, so an
# App installation token can never do it. Add the account as an OUTSIDE
# COLLABORATOR with Write, and give it an SSH key and NO API token: an SSH key
# reaches git refs and nothing else, which is the whole containment story.
# The email is GitHub's per-account no-reply form, so no real address is ever
# committed. Set all three or none — a partial set is a hard error, never a
# fallback to whatever identity is ambient. Unset → autofix actions are
# unavailable and the app renders them disabled with a reason. Every autofix
# machine holds a copy of this key, so each one added widens where a credential
# with push access to contributor branches lives.
# TRIAGE_PUSH_LOGIN=your-machine-user
# TRIAGE_PUSH_EMAIL=12345678+your-machine-user@users.noreply.github.com
# TRIAGE_PUSH_SSH_KEY_FILE=~/.ssh/your-machine-user

# Autofix worker (a sandbox machine — a separate switch from
# TRIAGE_VERIFY_WORKER so either can run alone). It drains the fix queue: merging
# the base branch in, rebasing off a conflict, or having an agent author a fix
# for a failing gate. Every other app instance serves the queue API only, so a
# click on a laptop reaches a runner. Any number of machines may run one — the
# queue claim is a compare-and-swap, and an approved change is rebuilt from the
# store on whichever machine pushes it. Each needs its own copy of the push key
# above, so weigh that against the redundancy before provisioning another.
# TRIAGE_FIX_AUTOPUSH names the actions that push on a clean compile preflight
# rather than parking for human review (comma-separated; valid: update, rebase,
# fix — empty means every action parks).
# TRIAGE_FIX_AUTOHUNT=1 additionally lets an idle worker queue eligible PRs
# itself, which together with autopush is an unattended backlog burn-down.
# TRIAGE_FIX_WORKER=1
# TRIAGE_FIX_AUTOPUSH=update
# TRIAGE_FIX_AUTOHUNT=1

# Prospector options. All OPTIONAL — each has a default.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# PROSPECTOR_OPERATOR=            # identity stamped on the activity log (default: git user.name)
# PROSPECTOR_LIVE_TTL_MIN=60      # live-overlay sweep TTL, minutes (how long GitHub state is cached)
# PROSPECTOR_FEEDBACK_REPO=       # repo the in-app feedback button files to (unset disables the button)
# PROSPECTOR_NO_LAUNCH_SWEEP=1    # disable the startup live-state sweep

# Dev server ports — auto-appended by setup.sh per worktree; override here.
# API_PORT=8787
# VITE_PORT=5173
